'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-05-03T23:26:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Winnie Hu and Luis Ferré-Sadurní', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Winnie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Luis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ferré-Sadurní', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/08f9df88-7633-58f2-b10a-e5b9370c96d6', 'word_count': 1765, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08f9df88-7633-58f2-b10a-e5b9370c96d6'}, {'abstract': "Biden doesn't rile up the grassroots. Critical race theory does.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/03/opinion/public-school-culture-wars.html', 'snippet': "Biden doesn't rile up the grassroots. Critical race theory does.", 'lead_paragraph': 'There is a quote from Ralph Reed that I often return to when trying to understand how the right builds political power. “I would rather have a thousand school board members than one president and no school board members,” the former leader of the Christian Coalition said in 1996. School board elections are a great training ground for national activism. They can pull parents, particularly mothers, into politics around intensely emotional issues, building a thriving grass roots and keeping it mobilized.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/04/opinion/03goldberg-lead/03goldberg-lead-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/04/opinion/03goldberg-lead/03goldberg-lead-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'capti