k': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stacy Cowley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stacy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88407e2f-96fb-58e0-ad31-a9f739385d15', 'word_count': 488, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88407e2f-96fb-58e0-ad31-a9f739385d15'}, {'abstract': 'Most politicians are hypocrites. Voters tend not to care.\xa0', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/04/opinion/republicans-biden-hypocrisy.html', 'snippet': 'Most politicians are hypocrites. Voters tend not to care.\xa0', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Biden is a tough man to vilify. Maybe it’s the grandfatherly vibe or the down-to-earth speaking style or all that talk of compassion and healing. Whatever the reason, Republicans have had little success thus far convincing Americans — beyond the alternative-reality MAGAverse, of course — that good old Uncle Joe is radical, corrupt or even a little bit scary.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/04/opinion/04cottle1/04cottle1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/04/opinion/04cottle1/04cottle1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/04/opinion/04cottle1/04cottle1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/04/opinion/04cottle1/04cottle1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 's