', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Fausset', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fausset', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/17d2fce3-3398-5bb6-9ebf-f6cb4bfddd5a', 'word_count': 1029, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17d2fce3-3398-5bb6-9ebf-f6cb4bfddd5a'}, {'abstract': 'Following Alex Trebek’s death, a parade of replacements seem to be battling for the soul of the game — and the state of knowledge itself.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/05/arts/television/jeopardy-trebek-new-host.html', 'snippet': 'Following Alex Trebek’s death, a parade of replacements seem to be battling for the soul of the game — and the state of knowledge itself.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a recent “Jeopardy!” episode, one of the contestants, Mike Nelson, alighted upon a once-in-a-lifetime opportunity. Nelson — “an actor originally from Chesterton, Indiana,” as the “Jeopardy!” announcer Johnny Gilbert introduced him — selected an $800 clue that triggered a Daily Double. It was Anderson Cooper’s first night guest-hosting the show, but Nelson had a different man in mind as he made his wager. “I’ve always wanted to say this,” Nelson said. “Let’s make it a true Daily Double” — and here Nelson closed his eyes and lifted his hands as if to signal for some kind of celestial field goal — “Alex.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Should the New ‘Jeopardy!’ Be? Guest Hosts Provide the Clues.', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Trying to Fill Alex Trebek’s Shoes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Jeopardy! (TV Program)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Couric, Katie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jennings, Ken', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oz, Mehmet C', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodgers, Aaron', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trebek, Alex', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Contests and Prizes', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Celebrities', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-05-05T16:26:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amanda Hess', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1fb515e-57e5-584c-a014-3279750797f9', 'word_count': 1647, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1fb515e-57e5-584c-a014-3279750797f9'}, {'abstract': 'Congress and the Biden administration are taking action aimed at sharply restricting two powerful planet-warming gases.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/05/climate/greenhouse-gas-emissions.html', 'snippet': 'Congress and the Biden administration are taking action aimed at sharply restricting two powerful planet-warming gases.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the past week, Congress and the Biden administration took their first concrete steps to reduce emissions of two dangerous greenhouse gases: Methane, which is emitted during  natural gas extraction and by leaks from oil and gas wells, and hydrofluorocarbons, which are used in refrigeration and air-conditioning systems.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/03/climate/03CLI-HFCS1a/m