ubject', 'value': 'Federal Courts (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fourteenth Amendment (US Constitution)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Constitution (US)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court (US)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Biden, Joseph R Jr', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boumediene, Lakhdar', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George W', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Randolph, Arthur Raymond', 'rank': 17, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rao, Neomi', 'rank': 18, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 19, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Guantanamo Bay Naval Base (Cuba)', 'rank': 20, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 21, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-05-06T09:00:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Linda Greenhouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Linda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenhouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e075c35-61ad-5de5-b858-a42f8074ca77', 'word_count': 1237, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e075c35-61ad-5de5-b858-a42f8074ca77'}, {'abstract': 'Nicholas Britell’s scores — for “Succession,” “Moonlight” and “The Underground Railroad,” among others — suggest whole new ways of writing for film and television.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/06/magazine/nicholas-britell.html', 'snippet': 'Nicholas Britell’s scores — for “Succession,” “Moonlight” and “The Underground Railroad,” among others — suggest whole new ways of writing for film and television.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first time I understood what it is that the composer Nicholas Britell does for a film — understood with my whole body — I was in his studio, listening to a mistake he had made and the way he had fixed it. Earlier, in a cafe off Lincoln Center, I had asked him about the process of making “Moonlight,” the Oscar-winning coming-of-age story he scored for Barry Jenkins. Britell told me about a scene, early in the film, in which the protagonist’s mentor teaches him to swim. “I was looking at the sequence like, ‘Oh, Juan and Little swim,’” Britell said. “It’s a beautiful moment. This will be something special he can carry with him.” So Britell wrote a sweet piece in F major, an orchestral swell with a clarinet singing a variation on Little’s theme on top. He played it for Jenkins. The response was a visceral “nope.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/09/magazine/09mag-britell/09mag-britell-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/09/magazine/09mag-britell/09mag-britell-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/09/magazine/09mag-britell/09mag-britell-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/09/magazine/09mag-britell/09mag-britell-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJ