, 'print_headline': 'Spacious and Quiet. No Brawls. Is This Actually My Commute?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transit Systems', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Commuting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Railroads', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Jersey Transit', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quarantine (Life and Culture)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-05-10T09:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Barry and Bryan Anselm', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Bryan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anselm', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/68c82efe-70dc-5c82-b5e2-4886ca1edb19', 'word_count': 896, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68c82efe-70dc-5c82-b5e2-4886ca1edb19'}, {'abstract': 'From tried-and-true botanical elixirs to lab-derived formulas, there’s a blend for everyone, whether it’s infused with rose hip, black currant or fermented buriti.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/10/t-magazine/best-face-oils.html', 'snippet': 'From tried-and-true botanical elixirs to lab-derived formulas, there’s a blend for everyone, whether it’s infused with rose hip, black currant or fermented buriti.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Few beauty products can safely claim to have been used for millenniums. Oils are among the exceptions. Cleopatra is said to have favored olive and sesame oils, while Pliny the Elder was partial to rose oil (mixed with lion’s fat), which, he wrote in “Natural History” (c. 77 A.D.), “protects the skin of the face from all kinds of spots.” Many of his contemporaries, no doubt, prized frankincense, 3,000 tons of which, as Ligaya Mishan writes in her recent essay for T’s Beauty & Luxury issue, were carried by camels across Arabia’s Rub’ al Khali every year.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/10/t-magazine/fashion/Beauty-extra-still-slide-IWM1-copy/Beauty-extra-still-slide-IWM1-copy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/10/t-magazine/fashion/Beauty-extra-still-slide-IWM1-copy/Beauty-extra-still-slide-IWM1-copy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/10/t-magazine/fashion/Beauty-extra-still-slide-IWM1-copy/Beauty-extra-still-slide-IWM1-copy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/10/t-magazine/fashion/Beauty-extra-still-slide-IWM1-copy/Beauty-extra-still-slide-IWM1-copy-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1215, 'width': 1824, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/10/t-magazine/fashion/Beauty-extra-still-slide-IWM1-copy/Beauty-extra-still-slide-IWM1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/05/10/t-magazine/fashion/Beauty-extra-still-slide-IWM1-copy/Beauty-extra-still-slide-IWM1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/10/t-magazine/fashion/Beauty-extra-still-slide-IWM1-copy/Beauty-extra-still-slide-IWM1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Best Face Oils to Use Now', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Face Oils; Cleopatra Approves', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Skin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oils and Fats', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cosmetics and Toiletries', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beauty (Concept)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Augustinus Bader (Cosmetics and Skincare)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pai Skincare Ltd', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Vintner's Daughter", 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maran, Josie', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodin, Linda', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-05-10T09:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marisa Meltzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Meltzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a44c0ab2-4bff-509c-bb65-74fdac5e0c49', 'word_count': 1078, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a44c0ab2-4bff-509c-bb65-74fdac5e0c49'}, {'abstract': 'In 19th-century Europe, delicate florals defined olfactory perfection. Then came the rise of bold, musky ‘oriental’ scents, which revealed much about the West’s perception of Eastern culture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/10/t-magazine/fragrance-perfume-orientalism.html', 'snippet': 'In 19th-century Europe, delicate florals defined olfactory perfection. Then came the rise of bold, musky ‘oriental’ scents, which revealed much about the West’s perception of Eastern culture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I REMEMBER AS IF it were yesterday that distant afternoon on which I first smelled oudh. I was in my grandmother’s house in Delhi. I was 13, maybe 14. We had a family perfumer, or attarwallah, a man of some refinement, who came to us from Lucknow — a city that is a metonym for high Indo-Islamic culture. We didn’t know the attarwallah’s name, or how he knew to follow us from address to change of address. But he came without fail two or three times a year. A slim, gliding figure, with a mouth reddened from paan, or betel leaf and areca nut, the attarwallah produced his wares from carved bottles of colored glass that he carried in a black leather doctor’s bag. He showed us scents according to which season we were in. So in winter, musk and patchouli; in summer, white-flowered varieties of jasmine — of which there are some 40 odd in India — as well as rose and vetiver. In the monsoon, he brought us mitti attar, which imitates the smell of parched earth exhaling after the first rain (“mitti” means “mud” in Hindi). The perfumes came from the medieval Indian town of Kannauj, which is a 75-mile drive west of Lucknow and which, like its French counterpart, Grasse, has a tradition of perfume manufacturing several centuries old. Once he had drawn his perfume out on white cotton buds at the tips of long, thin sticks, the attarwallah lingered over his customers, telling stories of the various scents and reciting the odd romantic couplet of Urdu poetry.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '72', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/12/t-magazine/12tmag-fragrance-slide-C6K1/12tmag-fragrance-slide-C6K1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/12/t-magazine/12tmag-fragrance-slide-C6K1/12tmag-fragrance-slide-C6K1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/12/t-magazine/12tmag-fragrance-slide-C6K1/12tmag-fragrance-slide-C6K1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 665, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/12/t-magazine/12tmag-fragrance-slide-C6K1/12tmag-fragrance-slide-C6K1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1330, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/12/t-magazine/12tmag-fragrance-slide-C6K1/12tmag-fragrance-slide-C6K1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/05/12/t-magazine/12tmag-fragrance-slide-C6K1/12tmag-fragrance-slide-C6K1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/12/t-magazine/12tmag-fragrance-slide-C6K1/12tmag-fragrance-slide-C6K1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Fragrances That Changed the Field', 'kicker': 'The 1980s', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Scent of Passion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Perfumes and Fragrances', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Said, Edward W', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smells and Odors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smell (Olfaction)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-05-10T09:00:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Aatish Taseer and Collier Schorr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aatish', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taseer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Collier', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schorr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e779808-5c53-56fa-b14a-15c497dc7476', 'word_count': 5277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e779808-5c53-56fa-b14a-15c497dc7476'}, {'abstract': 'Now that people of all genders have embraced the rituals of beauty, once seemingly reserved only for women, what does it mean to adorn our faces?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/10/t-magazine/men-makeup-gender-norms.html', 'snippet': 'Now that people of all genders have embraced the rituals of beauty, once seemingly reserved only for women, what does it mean to adorn our faces?', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE FIRST TIME I remember ever seeing a man wearing makeup was at a nightclub in midtown Kansas City, Mo., that allowed in under-21s one night a month. It must have been 1991 or ’92; he was out front with his friends, smoking; I, in my favorite blue-and-white star-print maxi dress and thrift store velveteen Mary Janes, was arriving with my brother and his girlfriend.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/10/t-magazine/10tmag-beautytoppers-slide-G5VH/10tmag-beautytoppers-slide-G5VH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/10/t-magazine/10tmag-beautyto