, 'url': 'images/2021/05/11/books/review/Weil1/Weil1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'It’s Time to Sweat It Out and Get Pumped With Alison Bechdel', 'kicker': 'Nonfiction', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pump It Up', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exercise', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sweating', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Clubs', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bechdel, Alison', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Secret to Superhuman Strength (Book)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Content Type: Personal Profile', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-05-12T09:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth Weil', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weil', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ceed0475-f607-52dc-be0a-e4bec368d8a6', 'word_count': 1386, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ceed0475-f607-52dc-be0a-e4bec368d8a6'}, {'abstract': 'These family style beef short ribs were inspired by a cross-cultural Texas childhood.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/12/magazine/ribs-texas-taiwan-recipe.html', 'snippet': 'These family style beef short ribs were inspired by a cross-cultural Texas childhood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Jennifer Hwa Dobbertin was small, she roamed her parents’ restaurant on Fredericksburg Road in San Antonio, Texas, cadging French fries from the diners. Her mom, Nancy Collet, was born in Taiwan and immigrated to the city on the arm of an American serviceman she later divorced. Collet cooked in the kitchen of Golden Wok, one of San Antonio’s oldest Chinese restaurants, before taking a job as a cocktail waitress and marrying a restaurant manager, Jim Dobbertin, Jennifer’s dad. In the 1980s, the couple opened an American-style diner, the Conglomeration. The menu ran to burgers, fried catfish, hush puppies. Collet offered blackened redfish for $9.50, fried okra for $1.50. “It was a disaster,” Dobbertin told me recently. “She could have been making Chinese food.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/16/magazine/16mag-eat/16mag-eat-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 451, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/04/16/magazine/16mag-eat/16mag-eat-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 451}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/16/magazine/16mag-eat/16mag-eat-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 770, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/16/magazine/16mag-eat/16mag-eat-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1541, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/16/magazine/16mag-eat/16mag-eat-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/04/16/magazine/16mag-eat/16mag-eat-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/16/magazine/16mag-eat/16mag-eat-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Authentically American Red-Cooked Short Ribs, by Way of Taiwan', 'kicker': 'Eat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Authentically American Red-Cooked Short Ribs, by Way of Taiwan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chinese Food (Cuisine)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beef', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Taiwan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-05-12T09:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Sifton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sifton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c21f005-4753-5d11-96ba-581d540325b0', 'word_count': 703, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c21f005-4753-5d11-96ba-581d540325b0'}, {'abstract': 'Many of them are vaccinated, but when it comes to their kids, the unknowns give them pause.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/12/parenting/vaccine-children.html', 'snippet': 'Many of them are vaccinated, but when it comes to their kids, the unknowns give them pause.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On May 4, Dr. Hina Talib, who goes by the handle @teenhealthdoc on Instagram, asked the parents among her 33,000 followers if they were hesitant to get the coronavirus vaccine for their 12- to 15-year-olds, and if so, why. Dr. Talib, who is a physician in the adolescent medicine division at Children’s Hospital at Montefiore in New York, was surprised to get 600 messages filled with questions and concerns.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/10/well/PAR-VACCINE-HESITANCY/merlin_185576892_6ced2b2c-6ef2-4b24-aa67-5469d33705da-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/10/well/PAR-VACCINE-HESITANCY/merlin_185576892_6ced2b2c-6ef2-4b24-aa67-5469d33705da-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/10/well/PAR-VACCINE-HESITANCY/merlin_185576892_6ced2b2c-6ef2-4b24-aa67-5469d33705da-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/10/well/PAR-VACCINE-HESITANCY/merlin_185576892_6ced2b2c-6ef2-4b24-aa67-5469d33705da-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1152, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/10/well/PAR-VACCINE-HESITANCY/PAR-VACCINE-HESITANCY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/05/10/well/PAR-VACCINE-HESITANCY/PAR-VACCINE-HESITANCY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/10/well/PAR-VACCINE-HESITANCY/PAR-VACCINE-HESITANCY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'They’re Not Anti-Vaccine, but These Parents Are Hesitant About the Covid Shot', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Some Parents Are Hesitant About Shots for Offspring', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vaccination and Immunization', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-05-12T09:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Parenting', 'section_name': 'Parenting', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jessica Grose', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grose', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ace45f4b-599f-5583-871a-bfa9e1a27bbe', 'word_count': 1175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ace45f4b-599f-5583-871a-bfa9e1a27bbe'}, {'abstract': 'Democrats could use a two-seat advantage in 2022. Republicans call it a power grab. Residents call it voting rights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/12/opinion/the-argument-dc-statehood.html', 'snippet': 'Democrats could use a two-seat advantage in 2022. Republicans call it a power grab. Residents call it voting rights.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The District of Columbia can almost taste statehood. Last month, House Democrats passed a bill that would make it the 51st state. This is the second time in history that such a legislation has been passed in the House. But it’s not only a question of representation: Making D.C. a state would add two probably Democratic senators and one Democratic representative, at a time when Democrats could use all the votes they can get. And Republicans aren’t willing to give in that easily.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/12/opinion/12argument-statehood-image/12argument-statehood-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/12/opinion/12argument-statehood-image/12argument-statehood-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/12/opinion/12argument-statehood-image/12argument-statehood-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 900, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/12/opinion/12argument-statehood-image/12argument-statehood-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 900, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/12/opinion/12argument-statehood-image/12argument-statehood-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/05/12/opinion/12argument-statehood-image/12argument-statehood-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/12/opinion/12argument-statehood-image/12argument-statehood-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'This Might Be the Year D.C. Becomes a State', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Statehood Movement for Washington, DC', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-05-12T09:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': "By ‘The Argument'", 'person': [], 'organization': "The Argument'"}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f5cde7f-3404-5239-8fee-e1b448d49914', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f5cde7f-3404-5239-8fee-e1b448d49914'}, {'abstract': 'How did it go? Well, you might say that American law enforcement didn’t exactly win any fans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/12/opinion/police-officers-usa-global.html', 'snippet': 'How did it go? Well, you might say that American law enforcement didn’t exactly win any fans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'American law enforcement is facing an intense — and long-overdue — public reckoning.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘I’m Speechless.’ We Asked Law-Enforcement Officers Around the World How American Policing Looks From Abroad.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Reform', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-05-12T09:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/1de5ebc5-0278-5df6-ba0c-2575c9144dc7', 'word_count': 223, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1de5ebc5-0278-5df6-ba0c-2575c9144dc7'}, {'abstract': 'Physical activity can promote a sense of purpose in life, creating a virtuous cycle that keeps you moving.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/12/well/move/exercise-mental-health-flourishing.html', 'snippet': 'Physical activity can promote a sense of purpose in life, creating a virtuous cycle that keeps you moving.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our exercise habits may influence our sense of purpose in life and our sense of purpose may affect how much we exercise, according to an interesting new study of the reciprocal effects of feeling your life has meaning and being often in motion. The study, which involved more than 18,000 middle-aged and older men and women, found that those with the most stalwart sense of purpose at the start were the most likely to become active over time, and vice versa.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/18/well/21PHYSED-PURPOSE/21PHYSED-PURPOSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/18/well/21PHYSED-PURPOSE/21PHYSED-PURPOSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/18/well/21PHYSED-PURPOSE/21PHYSED-PURPOSE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/18/well/21PHYSED-PURPOSE/21PHYSED-PURPOSE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/18/well/21PHYSED-PURPOSE/21PHYSED-PURPOSE-thumbStandard.jpg',