one, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Sanger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6fa2cc36-aece-56c7-b087-8ce4a3416863', 'word_count': 1467, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6fa2cc36-aece-56c7-b087-8ce4a3416863'}, {'abstract': 'As the president tries to assemble an anti-authoritarian alliance, he faces a choice: hypocrisy or realism?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/12/opinion/biden-democracy-alliance.html', 'snippet': 'As the president tries to assemble an anti-authoritarian alliance, he faces a choice: hypocrisy or realism?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Democracy is on the ropes, and President Biden says he wants to fight back. He plans to call a summit of democracies and to mobilize a broad coalition of democratic governments to contain the rise of authoritarian powers like China and Russia. That appears eminently sensible. It also resonates with the progressive moment in the United States, as many Americans are determined to repair their democracy, making it fairer and more inclusive.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/12/opinion/12krastev/12krastev-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/12/opinion/12krastev/12krastev-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/12/opinion/12krastev/12krastev-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'i