ent_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Dodd', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dodd', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/747c4aef-71ae-533b-84e9-955b6e7ea4a4', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/747c4aef-71ae-533b-84e9-955b6e7ea4a4'}, {'abstract': 'The writer and artist spent nearly three years researching why so many of us feel so alone. What she found was much worse than she imagined.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/15/books/review/kristen-radtke-loneliness.html', 'snippet': 'The writer and artist spent nearly three years researching why so many of us feel so alone. What she found was much worse than she imagined.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I started writing about loneliness by accident. It seems too simple to say I wrote about loneliness because I was often lonely, although I was. It was 2016, during an election cycle that left many of us feeling wrenched from reality, and suddenly I was seeing loneliness everywhere. I’d walk down the street or step onto the subway during my morning commute and find that I couldn’t stop watching people who were physically alone: someone eating a sloppy gyro on the curb while staring blankly into space, an exhausted nurse still in her scrubs nodding off on the bus, the bodega cashier scrolling absently through his phone before a backdrop of condoms and Fritos pinned to the wall. There was nothing verifiably lonely about any of these people, and the fact that I identified loneliness in them said a lot more about me than it did them. I wanted to see their loneliness because I was feeling it myself.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/23/books/review/12Radtke-SUB01/12Radtke-SUB01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 231, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/23/books/review/12Radtke-SUB01/12Radtke-SUB01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 231}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/23/books/review/12Radtke-SUB01/12Radtke-SUB01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 394, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/23/books/review/12Radtke-SUB01/12Radtke-SUB01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 788, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/23/books/review/12Radtke-SUB01/12Radtke-SUB01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/05/23/books/review/12Radtke-SUB01/12Radtke-SUB01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/23/books/review/12Radtke-SUB01/12Radtke-SUB01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Kristen Radtke Considers Another American Epidemic: Loneliness', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Loneliness Is an Emergency', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Radtke, Kristen (1987- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Loneliness', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-05-15T09:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kristen Radtke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kristen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Radtke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f4bd7e88-74fa-5e74-9dfa-0a41cd659a5b', 'word_count': 940, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f4bd7e88-74fa-5e74-9dfa-0a41cd659a5b'}, {'abstract': 'How a politician who died in 2003 continues to dominate much of today’s political discussion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/15/books/review/daniel-patrick-moynihan-was-often-right-joe-klein-on-why-it-still-matters.html', 'snippet': 'How a politician who died in 2003 continues to dominate much of today’s political discussion.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The central conservative truth is that it is culture, not politics, that determines the success of a society,” Senator Daniel Patrick Moynihan of New York said during a lecture at Harvard in 1986. “The central liberal truth is that politics can change a culture and save it from itself.” Moynihan, an apostle of complexity, lived at the intersection of those two truths, a place where he was free to become one of the most creative American thinkers of the late 20th century. He sensed, and then came to know, that the social problems of what was being called “postindustrial” society would be different from those that came before. He identified these problems, sometimes controversially. In so doing, he predicted the dislocations of the 21st century with uncanny accuracy. He did it with elegance and wit and — this may be a surprise — transcendent humility. His spot-on sense of what truly mattered deserves to be revisited now, if we’re to grope our way past the mess we’ve become as a society.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 