ename': None, 'lastname': 'Blas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Farchy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/3bcc6f22-f79d-536d-a23e-381d3a39b98b', 'word_count': 1487, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3bcc6f22-f79d-536d-a23e-381d3a39b98b'}, {'abstract': 'The depiction of empowered, loving Black women in “Run the World” should not feel radical, but it is. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/16/opinion/culture/Run-the-World-Black-Women-TV.html', 'snippet': 'The depiction of empowered, loving Black women in “Run the World” should not feel radical, but it is. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'Do you remember that scene in “Sex and the City” when Carrie gets dumped by her boyfriend via Post-it note? “I’m sorry,” it reads. “I can’t. Don’t hate me.” That’s what I think of when my friends ask if I’ve seen the latest TV show populated almost entirely by white people: I’m sorry. I can’t. Don’t hate me.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/16/opinion/16Jackson/16Jackson--articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/16/opinion/16Jackson/16Jackson--articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/16/opinion/16Jackson/16Jackson--jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/16/opinion/16Jackson/16Jackson--superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1200, 'width': 1800, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'cr