yt://interactive/697f81d5-b334-5569-906c-cb703c9323b6'}, {'abstract': 'Despite all assertions that the site isn’t powered by its sexual content, the platform is synonymous with porn. What is it really?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/18/magazine/onlyfans-porn.html', 'snippet': 'Despite all assertions that the site isn’t powered by its sexual content, the platform is synonymous with porn. What is it really?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gia the Smutty Mystic’s mornings resemble those of many people who work from home: She wakes up around 6 in her sunny apartment in the East Bay region of California and grabs her phone. Inevitably, there’s a backlog of messages from customers, most of which she responds to before she gets up to water her plants and wash her face. If it’s a Friday, when she usually records most of her weekly content — JOI (jerk-off instruction) videos are one of her current specialties — she has to work around the screams of children playing in a nearby park and traffic noise from the intersection outside her window. During the rest of the week, she’s seated at her desk by noon, editing, posting and queuing her content, which ranges from safe-for-work dancing videos to explicit boy-girl clips. Much of what she produces is shared on Instagram, Reddit and Twitter. But by 6 p.m., she’s “in the DMs,” meaning her private messages on OnlyFans, a subscription-based platform that allows people to charge for access to the materials they share. She’ll be there until midnight, sexting with customers one-on-one.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '52', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/23/magazine/23mag-onlyfans/23mag-onlyfans-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/23/magazine/23mag-onlyfans/23mag-onlyfans-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/23/magazine/23mag-onlyfans/23mag-onlyfans-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/23/magazine/23mag-onlyfans/23mag-onlyfans-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1200, 'width': 1800, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/23/magazine/23mag-onlyfans/23mag-onlyfans-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/05/23/magazine/23mag-onlyfans/23mag-onlyfans-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/23/magazine/23mag-onlyfans/23mag-onlyfans-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'OnlyFans Isn’t Just Porn ;)', 'kicker': 'The Money Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Web of Intimacy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prostitution', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'OnlyFans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pornography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Backpage.com', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Craigslist', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quarantine (Life and Culture)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-05-18T12:41:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charlotte Shane', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charlotte', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9f858a2-4953-53cf-8350-4fef18240e88', 'word_count': 4943, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9f858a2-4953-53cf-8350-4fef18240e88'}, {'abstract': 'The federal government is undertaking the largest stimulus program in American history. The payoff could be more widely shared than usual.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/18/magazine/stimulus-us-economy.html', 'snippet': 'The federal government is undertaking the largest stimulus program in American history. The payoff could be more widely shared than usual.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The plunge the U.S. economy took last spring was so precipitous that the charts from the time look, literally, like cliffs. Industrial output fell 12.7 percent in April 2020, the worst drop since records began a century earlier, as entire industries shut down virtually overnight. Airline travel, as measured by the number of people passing through T.S.A. checkpoints, fell 94 percent in a month — from two million people on March 1 to just 124,000 on April 1. In two months, employers cut 22 million jobs, more than in every recession in the last 50 years, combined.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '48', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/23/magazine/23Stimulus-image1/23Stimulus-image1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 743, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/23/magazine/23Stimulus-image1/23Stimulus-image1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 743}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/23/magazine/23Stimulus-image1/23Stimulus-image1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 827, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/23/magazine/23Stimulus-image1/23Stimulus-image1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1654, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/23/magazine/23Stimulus-image1/23Stimulus-image1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/05/23/magazine/23Stimulus-image1/23Stimulus-image1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/23/magazine/23Stimulus-image1/23Stimulus-image1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'America Is on a Road to a Better Economy. But Better for Whom?', 'kicker': 'The Money issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Road to Recovery', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recession and Depression', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stimulus (Economic)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-05-18T12:41:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Casselman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Casselman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5e8431f-066f-5aca-a0e1-343637b0ba4e', 'word_count': 3053, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5e8431f-066f-5aca-a0e1-343637b0ba4e'}, {'abstract': 'Custom design your structure based on the tree itself, and don’t hesitate to consult a professional when needed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/18/magazine/how-to-build-a-treehouse.html', 'snippet': 'Custom design your structure based on the tree itself, and don’t hesitate to consult a professional when needed.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Choose the right tree,” says Dustin Feider, whose company O2, based in Oakland, Calif., builds high-end treehouses for clients around the country, including a $500,000 one that he’s currently working on in Sonoma County. Feider’s designs tend to be modern and whimsical, like his pinecone-shaped, geodesic-glass treehouse suspended 50 feet up in a cluster of redwoods. His obsession with treehouses started when he was a child in Pewaukee, Wis., where he cobbled together his first structure up a poplar using scrap lumber pilfered from a nearby construction site. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/23/magazine/23mag-tip/23mag-tip-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/23/magazine/23mag-tip/23mag-tip-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url