': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/23/realestate/20HUNT-SIERRA9/merlin_187863183_e4e1a21b-6961-4be3-aa8d-ee49d4ca2cb0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/05/23/realestate/20HUNT-SIERRA9/merlin_187863183_e4e1a21b-6961-4be3-aa8d-ee49d4ca2cb0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/23/realestate/20HUNT-SIERRA9/merlin_187863183_e4e1a21b-6961-4be3-aa8d-ee49d4ca2cb0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'She Wanted a Post-Retirement Manhattan Pad for Under $900,000. Which Option Would You Choose?', 'kicker': 'The Hunt', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'With Family in New York, a Retiree Decides to Get Closer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Content Type: Personal Profile', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chelsea (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'West Village (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Financial District (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-05-20T09:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Real Estate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joyce Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joyce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/00316ea7-fc92-51c2-b86a-9864e2df20e5', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/00316ea7-fc92-51c2-b86a-9864e2df20e5'}, {'abstract': 'Richard Blanco uses English words and German inspiration to make sense of what these United States of America is worth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/20/magazine/poem-and-so-we-all-fall-down.html', 'snippet': 'Richard Blanco uses English words and German inspiration to make sense of what these United States of America is worth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Listen, I have Anselm Kiefer’s “The Language of the Birds” tattooed across my back. Kiefer is the artist who makes the world come to life for me. And so Blanco, using polysyndeton (joining successive words with conjunctions) and Kiefer’s “Steigend steigend sinke neider” to make sense of what these United States of America is worth, is a lovely thing. What’d he say? “And so the hope in what they let us hope: that/our ideals won’t all disappear.” Imagine saying out loud that we have ideals that matter, and living up to that in the moment. Blanco’s collection is called “How to Love a Country.” A challenging thing indeed, but one that has always mattered. Selected by Reginald Dwayne Betts', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/23/magazine/23mag-poem/23mag-poem-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/23/magazine/23mag-poem/23mag-poem-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/23/magazine/23mag-poem/23mag-poem-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url':