 'nyt://article/1eef6c7c-f656-575a-8b94-04b4d97b2097', 'word_count': 292, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1eef6c7c-f656-575a-8b94-04b4d97b2097'}, {'abstract': 'This film from Anders Refn shows how a Danish family in the 1940s justifies its complicity with the Nazis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/20/movies/into-the-darkness-review-making-excuses-for-collaboration.html', 'snippet': 'This film from Anders Refn shows how a Danish family in the 1940s justifies its complicity with the Nazis.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Into the Darkness” follows an industrialist’s family in early-1940s Denmark as its members rationalize their complicity with the occupying Nazis. Through politeness and denial, they variously decide aiding fugitives is too risky, that demonstrations are irresponsible, that they’re less pro-Nazi than anti-Communist and that doing business with the Germans is acceptable if it keeps the factory open. Also, while it’s distasteful that a German naval commander has designs on the industrialist’s daughter (he gives her a U-boat pin with a swastika on it), he’s not necessarily a party member. Right?', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/19/arts/into1/merlin_187713615_b1c98b4d-17cf-40dd-9aea-64b071a2a03b-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/19/arts/into1/merlin_187713615_b1c98b4d-17cf-40dd-9aea-64b071a2a03b-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/19/arts/into1/merlin_187713615_b1c98b4d-17cf-40dd-9aea-64b071a2a03b-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/19/arts/into1/merlin_187713615_b1c98b4d-17cf-40dd-9aea-64b071a2a03b-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1080, 'width': 1920, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/19/arts/into1/into1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/05/19/arts/into1/into1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/19/arts/into1/into1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Into the Darkness’ Review: Making Excuses for Collaboration', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Into the Darkness', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Into the Darkness (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Refn, Anders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-05-20T11:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Kenigsberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kenigsberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3892223-b9ae-5feb-a628-fedb044b64f5', 'word_count': 315, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3892223-b9ae-5feb-a628-fedb044b64f5'}, {'abstract': 'An alienated teenager finds questionable companionship in this playful coming-of-age romance by Suzanne Lindon, who was in her teens when she wrote, directed and starred in the film.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/20/movies/spring-blossom-review.html', 'snippet': 'An alienated teenager finds questionable companionship in this playful coming-of-age romance by Suzanne Lindon, who was in her teens when she wrote, directed and starred in the film.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the opening scene of “Spring Blossom,” a teenage brunette with downcast eyes spaces out as her peers prattle on around her. Suzanne (Suzanne Lindon) is not interested in what her fellow students have to say, yet her options for companionship are limited. In her humdrum existence — an endless back and forth from home to school flecked with the occasional unfulfilling party — it’s her fantasy life that sustains her.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/21/arts/spring1/merlin_187714290_d1d8fe76-5270-4f3d-941f-319144d84578-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/21/arts/spring1/merlin_187714290_d1d8fe76-5270-4f3d-941f-319144d84578-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/21/arts/spring1/merlin_187714290_d1d8fe76-5270-4f3d-941f-319144d84578-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/21/arts/spring1/merlin_187714290_d1d8fe76-5270-4f3d-941f-319144d84578-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1152, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/21/arts/spring1/spring1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbn