lastname': 'Hauser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ruth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fremson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a006f68-acbd-5a5a-93d2-ac9942044954', 'word_count': 1637, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a006f68-acbd-5a5a-93d2-ac9942044954'}, {'abstract': 'Rowan Renee’s new installation; Darrel Ellis’s self-portraits; Lu Yang’s digital world; Kunle Martins’s charcoal portraits; and Keltie Ferris’s exuberant abstractions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/20/arts/design/5-art-gallery-shows-to-see-right-now.html', 'snippet': 'Rowan Renee’s new installation; Darrel Ellis’s self-portraits; Lu Yang’s digital world; Kunle Martins’s charcoal portraits; and Keltie Ferris’s exuberant abstractions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Of the 44 artists featured in the 2020 exhibition “Marking Time: Art in the Age of Mass Incarceration,” at MoMA PS1, most produced work while serving prison sentences. Rowan Renee was an exception. Renee, who identifies as genderqueer and uses nonbinary pronouns, is the child of a prisoner. Their father was a convicted pedophile who died in jail, and Renee’s installation, “No Spirit for Me,” consisted almost entirely of related court and police legal documents that the artist had painstakingly recomposed as lithographs on handwoven fabric panels. Together the panels revealed the official history of one crime, but only hinted at the history of another, namely the abuse Renee had experienced as a child at their father’s hands.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/21/arts/19galleries-5NEW/19galleries-5NEW-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 696, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'ar