e': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/22fab202-0525-5a93-8855-a2a422102812', 'word_count': 861, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22fab202-0525-5a93-8855-a2a422102812'}, {'abstract': 'O’Farrell discusses her novel about Shakespeare and his family, and Judith Shulevitz talks about Rachel Cusk’s “Second Place.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/21/books/review/podcast-hamnet-maggie-ofarrell-judith-shulevitz-rachel-cusk-second-place.html', 'snippet': 'O’Farrell discusses her novel about Shakespeare and his family, and Judith Shulevitz talks about Rachel Cusk’s “Second Place.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maggie O’Farrell’s “Hamnet,” one of last year’s most widely acclaimed novels, imagines the life of William Shakespeare, his wife, Anne (or Agnes) Hathaway, and the couple’s son Hamnet, who died at 11 years old in 1596. On this week’s podcast, O’Farrell says she always planned for the novel to have the ensemble cast it does, but that her deepest motivation was the desire to capture a sense of the young boy at its center. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/10/books/review/Brooks2/Brooks2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 878, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/10/books/review/Brooks2/Brooks2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 878}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/10/books/review/Brooks2/Brooks2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 700, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/10/books/review/Brooks2/Brooks2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1400, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumb