//www.nytimes.com/2021/05/27/magazine/poem-len-bias-a-bouquet-of-flowers-and-ms-brooks.html', 'snippet': 'A moment between legends.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Len Bias dropped buckets in Frederick Douglass’s home state, where I was raised. And it’s startling to find that one night, instead of softly putting that orange globe in the bucket, he was handing flowers to Ms. Gwendolyn Brooks, who was doing a reading at the University of Maryland. Bias stopped by for a second with flowers — Collier reminds us of it all, with this Golden Shovel, borrowing his end words from “she kisses her killed boy,” a line in Brooks’s “The Last Quatrain of a Ballad for Emmett Till,” reminding us that while “it is difficult to get the news from poems ...” Selected by Reginald Dwayne Betts', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/30/magazine/30mag-poem/30mag-poem-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/30/magazine/30mag-poem/30mag-poem-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/30/magazine/30mag-poem/30mag-poem-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/30/magazine/30mag-poem/30mag-poem-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/30/magazine/30mag-poem/30mag-poem-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/05/30/magazine/30mag-poem/30mag-poem-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/30/magazine/30mag-poem/30mag-poem-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Poem: Len Bias, a Bouquet of Flowers, and Ms. Brooks', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Collier, Michael (Poet)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-05-27T09:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Collier and Reginald Dwayne Betts', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Reginald', 'middlename': 'Dwayne', 'lastname': 'Betts', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e1c045c-512f-5c93-ba51-61c0eb3204d0', 'word_count': 269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e1c045c-512f-5c93-ba51-61c0eb3204d0'}, {'abstract': 'In his latest Graphic Content column, Ed Park looks at Barry Windsor-Smith’s “Monsters” and Margaret Kimball’s “And Now I Spill the Family Secrets.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/27/books/review/monsters-barry-windsor-smith-and-now-i-spill-the-family-secrets-margaret-kimball.html', 'snippet': 'In his latest Graphic Content column, Ed Park looks at Barry Windsor-Smith’s “Monsters” and Margaret Kimball’s “And Now I Spill the Family Secrets.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'For this column, I’ve written in the past about Chris Ware’s “Rusty Brown” (356 pages, 18 years in the making, and weighing in at 3.5 pounds), Seth’s “Clyde Fans” (478 pages, 20 years, 3.15 pounds) and Jason Lutes’s “Berlin” (580, 22, 3.8). Add to the list MONSTERS (Fantagraphics, $39.99, 4.1 pounds), a 35-year labor by the British cartoonist Barry Windsor-Smith, and you have nearly a century’s worth of cartooning in just four books. For 365 large-format, black-and-white pages, Windsor-Smith conveys gruesome body horror and tender family scenes, nightmarish doom and quiet moments of connection. What starts off in the viscera-rich world of the gory EC comics of the ’50s morphs into a subtle exploration of memory, as the monster of the book’s title escapes from the lab, treks to his childhood home and listens to ghosts re-enact the tragedy of his youth for over 100 pages. Then things get really twisted.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/30/books/review/30Park-GraphicContent/30Park-GraphicContent-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1113, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/30/books/review/30Park-GraphicContent/30Park-GraphicContent-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1113}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/30/books/review/30Park-GraphicContent/30Park-GraphicContent-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 552, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/30/books/review/30Park-GraphicContent/30Park-GraphicContent-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1963, 'width': 1058, 'subType':