e': 'Morris, Errol', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-05-28T14:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SpecialSections', 'section_name': 'Special Series', 'byline': {'original': 'By Errol Morris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Errol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/59923403-94c0-59b0-87c9-168209f335f9', 'word_count': 829, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59923403-94c0-59b0-87c9-168209f335f9'}, {'abstract': 'These shows will help you to navigate whatever complex feelings you’re having about the world reopening, and ease you back into society at your own pace.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/28/arts/podcasts-anxiety-covid.html', 'snippet': 'These shows will help you to navigate whatever complex feelings you’re having about the world reopening, and ease you back into society at your own pace.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We have collectively lived through a global trauma that’s going to take years, maybe even decades, to process. With vaccination rates in the United States rising and infection rates plummeting, most of the Covid-19 restrictions we’ve become used to are disappearing, while all the forbidden fruits we’ve learned to live without — from indoor dining to air travel to large gatherings — are once again within our grasps.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/30/arts/30podcasts/30podcasts-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/30/arts/30podcasts/30podcasts-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'captio