jor': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quarantine (Life and Culture)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cellular Telephones', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-05-29T09:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hannah Selinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hannah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Selinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5ff7aeb-aa2b-5507-af00-4ad47f2a1436', 'word_count': 926, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5ff7aeb-aa2b-5507-af00-4ad47f2a1436'}, {'abstract': 'A recent firing at The Associated Press is the latest example of the way in which our digital pasts are never far from the present, despite what early internet evangelists thought.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/29/technology/emily-wilder-firing-ap.html', 'snippet': 'A recent firing at The Associated Press is the latest example of the way in which our digital pasts are never far from the present, despite what early internet evangelists thought.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The internet is a fossil machine. It preserves our thoughts, our political positions, our jokes, our photos, our triumphs and our mistakes in silicon amber, just waiting to be dug up. And that has led to a kind of modern sport: Find an outrageous piece of a person’s past that can be weaponized, put it on display for all to see and hope for the worst.', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/30/business/30web-trail-illo/30web-trail-illo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 436, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'imag