 lawyers for Theranos. As noted in the book, the firm was hired around the time Theranos learned that a Wall Street Journal reporter was looking into Theranos.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections: May 30, 2021', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2021-05-30T03:19:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Corrections', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Correction', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5ae53b8-86f2-58e9-a93a-282ba00b1b04', 'word_count': 411, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5ae53b8-86f2-58e9-a93a-282ba00b1b04'}, {'abstract': 'You can’t go wrong with a gin and tonic or a caipirinha. But many summer cocktails could stand a spark of whimsy without compromising their honest appeal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/29/at-home/summer-drink-recipes.html', 'snippet': 'You can’t go wrong with a gin and tonic or a caipirinha. But many summer cocktails could stand a spark of whimsy without compromising their honest appeal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like moving turtleneck sweaters to the back of the closet, it’s time to retire well-aged whiskeys and bring on the clear spirits, fruit juices and crushed ice. The warmer seasons demand refreshing drinks, alcoholic or not, served in tall, frosted glasses.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/30/multimedia/30ah-coolers1/merlin_188253867_acb3eef8-762e-4f66-a4b1-37e1e694ed93-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/30/multimedia/30ah-coolers1/merlin_188253867_acb3eef8-762e-4f66-a4b1-37e1e694ed93-artic